complete the squares as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-9{x}^{2}+54x-32y & =53 \\\\ (4{y}^{2}-32y)+(-9{x}^{2}+54x) & =53 \\\\ 4[{y}^{2}-8y]-9[{x}^{2}-6x] & =53 \\\\ 4[(y-4)^{2}-16]-9[(x-3)^{2}-9] & =53 \\\\ 4(y-4)^{2}-64-9(x-3)^{2}+81 & =53 \\\\ 4(y-4)^{2}-9(x-3)^{2} & =53+64-81 \\\\ 4(y-4)^{2}-9(x-3)^{2} & =36 \\\\ \\frac{4(y-4)^{2}}{36}-\\frac{9(x-3)^{2}}{36} & =\\frac{36}{36} \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotes of a vertical hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(3,4),\\,a=\\sqrt{9}=3,\\,b=\\sqrt{4}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\ny-4=\\pm \\frac{3}{2}(x-3).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060522/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9f652b753b77fc91fa788e0352f8f45171f84dce87b3831152801507b6c9d9e"} {"question" "q-83909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060522/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9d5154da0cfce74ec086f94646f720f625915de51a24fa5b3c3e0f11658bcaa4"} {"question" "q-83909" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060522/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9f652b753b77fc91fa788e0352f8f45171f84dce87b3831152801507b6c9d9e"} {"question" "q-83909" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y+4=\\pm \\frac{3}{2}(x+3)"] ["math" "y-3=\\pm \\frac{2}{3}(x-4)"] ["math" "y-3=\\pm \\frac{3}{2}(x-4)"] ["math" "y-4=\\pm \\frac{2}{3}(x+3)"] ["math" "y-4=\\pm \\frac{3}{2}(x-3)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060522/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9f652b753b77fc91fa788e0352f8f45171f84dce87b3831152801507b6c9d9e"} {"question" "q-83909" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y-4=\\pm \\frac{3}{2}(x-3)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060522/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9f652b753b77fc91fa788e0352f8f45171f84dce87b3831152801507b6c9d9e"} {"question" "q-165482" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-8y-4x-16=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060522/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9f652b753b77fc91fa788e0352f8f45171f84dce87b3831152801507b6c9d9e"} {"question" "q-165482" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group the $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-8y-4x-16 & =0 \\\\ 4{y}^{2}-8y-{x}^{2}-4x-16 & =0 \\\\ 4[{y}^{2}-2y]-[{x}^{2}+4x]-16 & =0 \\\\ 4[(y-1)^{2}-1]-[(x+2)^{2}-4]-16 & =0 \\\\ 4(y-1)^{2}-4-(x+2)^{2}+4-16 & =0 \\\\ 4(y-1)^{2}-(x+2)^{2} & =16 \\\\ \\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{16}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotes of a vertical hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(-2,1),\\,a=\\sqrt{4}=2,\\,b=\\sqrt{16}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\ny-1=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}(x+2).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060522/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9f652b753b77fc91fa788e0352f8f45171f84dce87b3831152801507b6c9d9e"} {"question" "q-165482" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060522/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9d5154da0cfce74ec086f94646f720f625915de51a24fa5b3c3e0f11658bcaa4"} {"question" "q-165482" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060522/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9f652b753b77fc91fa788e0352f8f45171f84dce87b3831152801507b6c9d9e"} {"question" "q-165482" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y+1=\\pm \\frac{1}{4}(x-2)"] ["math" "y+2=\\pm 2(x-1)"] ["math" "y+2=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}(x-1)"] ["math" "y-1=\\pm 4(x+2)"] ["math" "y-1=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}(x+2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060522/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9f652b753b77fc91fa788e0352f8f45171f84dce87b3831152801507b6c9d9e"} {"question" "q-165482" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y-1=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}(x+2)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060522/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9f652b753b77fc91fa788e0352f8f45171f84dce87b3831152801507b6c9d9e"} {"question" "q-10480" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $4{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2}-48y-10x+59=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060522/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9f652b753b77fc91fa788e0352f8f45171f84dce87b3831152801507b6c9d9e"} {"question" "q-10480" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group the $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2}-48y-10x+59 & =0 \\\\ 4{y}^{2}-48y-5{x}^{2}-10x+59 & =0 \\\\ 4[{y}^{2}-12y]-5[{x}^{2}+2x]+59 & =0 \\\\ 4[(y-6)^{2}-36]-5[(x+1)^{2}-1]+59 & =0 \\\\ 4(y-6)^{2}-144-5(x+1)^{2}+5+59 & =0 \\\\ 4(y-6)^{2}-5(x+1)^{2} & =144-5-59 \\\\ 4(y-6)^{2}-5(x+1)^{2} & =80 \\\\ \\frac{(y-6)^{2}}{20}-\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotes of a vertical hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(-1,6),\\,a=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5},\\,b=\\sqrt{16}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\ny-6=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}(x+1).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060522/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9f652b753b77fc91fa788e0352f8f45171f84dce87b3831152801507b6c9d9e"} {"question" "q-10480" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060522/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9d5154da0cfce74ec086f94646f720f625915de51a24fa5b3c3e0f11658bcaa4"} {"question" "q-10480" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060522/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9f652b753b77fc91fa788e0352f8f45171f84dce87b3831152801507b6c9d9e"} {"question" "q-10480" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y+1=\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}(x-6)"] ["math" "y+1=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}(x-6)"] ["math" "y-6=\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}(x+1)"] ["math" "y-6=\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}(x+1)"] ["math" "y-6=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}(x+1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060522/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9f652b753b77fc91fa788e0352f8f45171f84dce87b3831152801507b6c9d9e"} {"question" "q-10480" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y-6=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}(x+1)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060522/state.json" "file_sha256" "e9f652b753b77fc91fa788e0352f8f45171f84dce87b3831152801507b6c9d9e"} {"question" "e-5359" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\dfrac{(x+2)^2}{18} - \\dfrac{(y+7)^2}{32} = 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060522/example-5359.json" "file_sha256" "5fe0d06539e87c00fa9ffc72c74f803b588d261939e7ece61b1b74b04c984935"} {"question" "e-5359" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The standard equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^2}{a^2} - \\frac{(y-k)^2}{b^2}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a horizontal hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\dfrac{b}{a}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k) = (-2,-7),\\qquad a=\\sqrt{18} = 3\\sqrt{2}, \\qquad b=\\sqrt{32} = 4\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+7 & =\\pm (\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{2}})(x+2) \\\\ y+7 & =\\pm \\frac{4}{3}(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060522/example-5359.json" "file_sha256" "5fe0d06539e87c00fa9ffc72c74f803b588d261939e7ece61b1b74b04c984935"} {"question" "e-7430" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\dfrac{(y - 1) ^ 2} {16} - \\dfrac{(x + 3)^2}{9} = 1$." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060522/example-7430.json" "file_sha256" "15350db9f9bb9d33e3b8f23b4aedaa541f44bd2ab6a3f73a2aed9f5f24571365"} {"question" "e-7430" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{b^2} = 1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k = \\pm\\dfrac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\na = \\sqrt{16} = 4, \\qquad b = \\sqrt{9} = 3, \\qquad h = -3, \\qquad k = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =\\pm \\frac{4}{3}(x-(-3)) \\\\ y-1 & =\\pm \\frac{4}{3}(x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060522/example-7430.json" "file_sha256" "15350db9f9bb9d33e3b8f23b4aedaa541f44bd2ab6a3f73a2aed9f5f24571365"} {"question" "e-7428" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $9x^2 - y^2 - 36 x - 2y + 26 = 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060522/example-7428.json" "file_sha256" "96d818ceff8478953a2a98047771678e4fe0a83a2a523279e31845697663e445"} {"question" "e-7428" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group the $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-36x-2y+26 & =0 \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-36x-{y}^{2}-2y+26 & =0 \\\\ 9[{x}^{2}-4x]-[{y}^{2}+2y]+26 & =0 \\\\ 9[(x-2)^{2}-4]-[(y+1)^{2}-1]+26 & =0 \\\\ 9(x-2)^{2}-36-(y+1)^{2}+1+26 & =0 \\\\ 9(x-2)^{2}-(y+1)^{2} & =-26-1+36 \\\\ 9(x-2)^{2}-(y+1)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{9}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotes of the horizontal hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\ny-k = \\pm\\dfrac{b}{a}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nh = 2, \\qquad k = -1, \\qquad a = \\sqrt{1} = 1,\\qquad b = \\sqrt{9} = 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =\\pm 3(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060522/example-7428.json" "file_sha256" "96d818ceff8478953a2a98047771678e4fe0a83a2a523279e31845697663e445"} {"question" "e-7431" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $y^2 - x^2 - 2y = 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060522/example-7431.json" "file_sha256" "d3f0f9798d3139542d99316c3b11dcdd392ca1c8c53dea02e6622fdd3195375a"} {"question" "e-7431" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group the $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-2y & =0 \\\\ ({y}^{2}-2y)-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}-1-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}-{x}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotes of the vertical hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\dfrac{a}{b}(x-h) .\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k) = (0,1), \\qquad a = \\sqrt{1} = 1, \\qquad b= \\sqrt{1} = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =\\pm x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060522/example-7431.json" "file_sha256" "d3f0f9798d3139542d99316c3b11dcdd392ca1c8c53dea02e6622fdd3195375a"}] "usage" {"q-83906" [] "q-16443" [] "e-7431" [] "e-7430" [] "e-7428" [] "e-5359" []} "basis" 1953 "retractions" [[17592186092780 :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $16x^{2} - y^{2} - 64x - 2y + 47 = 0$."] [17592186092528 :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $x^{2} - y^{2} + 8x + 7 = 0$."] [17592186091999 :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\dfrac{(x+2)^2}{18} - \\dfrac{(y+7)^2}{32} = 1$."] [17592186091999 :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^2}{a^2} - \\frac{(y-k)^2}{b^2}=1\n$$\n\nFor a horizontal hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\dfrac{b}{a}(x-h)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n(h,k) = (-2,-7),\\qquad a=\\sqrt{18} = 3\\sqrt{2}, \\qquad b=\\sqrt{32} = 4\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny + 7 &= ± (\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{2}})(x + 2) \\\\\ny + 7 &= ± \\frac{4}{3}(x + 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186092072 :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k = \\pm\\dfrac{a}{b}(x-h)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\na = \\sqrt{16} = 4, \\qquad b = \\sqrt{9} = 3, \\qquad h = -3, \\qquad k = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - 1 &= ± \\frac{4}{3}(x - (-3)) \\\\\ny - 1 &= ± \\frac{4}{3}(x + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186092071 :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $9x^2 - y^2 - 36 x - 2y + 26 = 0$."] [17592186092071 :question/worked-solution "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group the $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9x^{2} - y^{2} - 36x - 2y + 26 &= 0 \\\\\n9x^{2} - 36x - y^{2} - 2y + 26 &= 0 \\\\\n9[x^{2} - 4x] - [y^{2} + 2y] + 26 &= 0 \\\\\n9[(x - 2)^{2} - 4] - [(y + 1)^{2} - 1] + 26 &= 0 \\\\\n9(x - 2)^{2} - 36 - (y + 1)^{2} + 1 + 26 &= 0 \\\\\n9(x - 2)^{2} - (y + 1)^{2} &= -26 - 1 + 36 \\\\\n9(x - 2)^{2} - (y + 1)^{2} &= 9 \\\\\n&= (x - 2)^{2} - ((y + 1)^{2})/(9) = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotes of the horizontal hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\ny-k = \\pm\\dfrac{b}{a}(x-h)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nh = 2, \\qquad k = -1, \\qquad a = \\sqrt{1} = 1,\\qquad b = \\sqrt{9} = 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny + 1 &= ± 3(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186092073 :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $y^2 - x^2 - 2y = 0$."] [17592186092073 :question/worked-solution "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group the $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} - x^{2} - 2y &= 0 \\\\\n(y^{2} - 2y) - x^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n(y - 1)^{2} - 1 - x^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n(y - 1)^{2} - x^{2} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotes of the vertical hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\dfrac{a}{b}(x-h)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n(h,k) = (0,1), \\qquad a = \\sqrt{1} = 1, \\qquad b= \\sqrt{1} = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - 1 &= ± x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186089605 17592186089606 17592186089607 17592186089608 17592186089609 17592186091146 17592186091147 17592186091148 17592186091149 17592186091150] "immutable_fields" [[17592186089604 :answer-field/correct] [17592186089604 :answer-field/id] [17592186089604 :answer-field/key] [17592186089604 :answer-field/type] [17592186091145 :answer-field/correct] [17592186091145 :answer-field/id] [17592186091145 :answer-field/key] [17592186091145 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
